Convert the child's Windows exit code to a POSIX exit status.
timeout returned the child's 32-bit exit code as its own status, which
was then cut to its low byte. A child terminated as if by a signal has
the signal in the top byte and zero elsewhere, so it gave status 0:
"timeout 5 sh -c 'kill -9 $$'" succeeded. Pass the code through
exit_code_to_posix(), as other spawning applets do, so such a child
gives 128+SIG, a crash such as 0xC0000005 gives 139, and any other code
above 255 is reported on stderr as the shell reports it.

With -k, a child that exited during the grace period also replaced the
status 124 (137 with -s KILL) with its own, typically the signal in
the top byte, so a timed out command reported success. Keep 124 or
137 in that case, as GNU timeout does.

---
 coreutils/timeout.c |    7 +++++--
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/coreutils/timeout.c
+++ b/coreutils/timeout.c
@@ -103,6 +103,7 @@
 #else
 	intptr_t ret;
 	DWORD status = EXIT_SUCCESS;
+	DWORD code;
 #endif
 	duration_t timeout;
 	duration_t kill_timeout;
@@ -208,8 +209,10 @@
 
 	child = (HANDLE)ret;
 	atexit(kill_child);
-	if (timeout_wait(timeout, child, &status))
+	if (timeout_wait(timeout, child, &code)) {
+		status = exit_code_to_posix(code);
 		goto finish;
+	}
 	status = signo == SIGKILL ? 137 : 124;
 
 	pid = (pid_t)GetProcessId(child);
@@ -217,7 +220,7 @@
 		kill(pid, signo);
 
 		if (kill_timeout > 0) {
-			if (timeout_wait(kill_timeout, child, &status))
+			if (timeout_wait(kill_timeout, child, &code))
 				goto finish;
 			kill(pid, SIGKILL);
 			status = 137;
